CloudBurrow patch 35 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1061; the bug: #1437).

The bug: googlesqlite v0.3.1 refused INTERVAL arithmetic, which BigQuery
documents: INTERVAL + INTERVAL failed "unsupported add operator for
interval value", as did INTERVAL - INTERVAL, INTERVAL * INT64 (and INT64 *
INTERVAL), INTERVAL / INT64 and -INTERVAL. DATE, DATETIME and TIMESTAMP +/-
INTERVAL let time.Date normalise every field at once, so DATE '2021-01-31'
+ INTERVAL 1 MONTH was 2021-03-03 where BigQuery has 2021-02-28, and
INTERVAL + DATE failed. JUSTIFY_DAYS, JUSTIFY_HOURS and JUSTIFY_INTERVAL
changed their argument in place, JUSTIFY_HOURS carried minutes into hours
by the hours, and none made the signs of adjacent parts agree.
MAKE_INTERVAL kept parts out of INTERVAL's range, and EXTRACT read the
parts as given, so EXTRACT(MINUTE FROM MAKE_INTERVAL(minute => 72)) was
72. A DATE, DATETIME or TIMESTAMP difference went through time.Duration,
which overflows past 292 years.

The patch does these as GoogleSQL does (googlesql/public/interval_value.cc
and functions/date_time_util.cc): an interval is months, days and
nanoseconds, each with its own sign and range (10,000 years of months,
3,660,000 days, 3,660,000 days of nanoseconds); arithmetic works part by
part; division carries the months' remainder into days at 30 a month and
the days' into the time part at 24 hours a day, truncated to microseconds;
adding an interval to a date or time adds the months first (clamping the
day to the month's end), then the days, then the time part. A part out of
range is GoogleSQL's error ("Interval field months '120001' is out of
range -120000 to 120000", "Interval overflow during multiplication",
"Interval division by zero"), and a date or time out of range is an error.
interval_arithmetic_test.go fails without the patch and passes with it;
the module's value_interval_test.go, which held the arithmetic
unsupported, now holds its results.

